WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
OAuth 2.0 is primarily a delegated authorization framework.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:11.320
It lets a client obtain authorization to access a resource, usually through an authorization server.

3
00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:16.780
OpenID Connect adds authentication and identity information on top of OAuth.

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.400
The distinction matters.

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.700
OAuth answers questions about delegated access.

6
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:29.560
OpenID Connect adds a way for an application to learn who the user is through an identity token and standardized identity flows.

7
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.320
The authorization code flow is a common pattern for web applications.

8
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:45.140
At a high level, the user is redirected to the identity provider, authenticates there, consents or satisfies policy, and the application receives an authorization code through a redirect.

9
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.000
The application exchanges that code for tokens through a back-channel request.

10
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:59.340
Client identifiers, redirect URIs, scopes, state, and, in many modern cases, PKCE all help constrain the flow.

11
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:07.040
Developers do not need to memorize every protocol field, but they do need to use well-maintained libraries and configure them correctly.

12
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:25.400
Common implementation mistakes include accepting unregistered redirect URIs, failing to validate state, confusing access tokens with ID tokens, trusting unvalidated claims, storing tokens insecurely, requesting excessive scopes, or assuming federation automatically grants local authorization.

13
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:36.700
Identity provider integration should be explicit about which provider is trusted, which client is allowed, what token audience is expected, and how external identities map to local accounts.

14
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:45.680
Federation simplifies authentication only when the application validates the protocol outcome and enforces its own authorization rules.
